            <!-- Online Search Section -->
            <section class="content-section">
                <h2>Search Court Records in Miami-Dade</h2>
                <p>The <a href="https://www2.miamidadeclerk.gov/QOS/">Miami-Dade court records search portal</a> lets you look up cases by name or case number. Guest users can view basic case info at no cost. Registration gives you more search options, such as booking number, date of birth, or arrest date searches. Advanced access to civil, family, and probate court records follows the Florida Supreme Court Standards for Access to Electronic Court Records and the Access Security Matrix. This service is free in Miami-Dade County.</p>

                <p>Each search unit for Official Records costs $1.00 in Miami-Dade County. You buy units in advance and use one per search. If you do not know the year or case number, the clerk charges $2 per year per name to search court records. Certified copies cost $7 each. These fees apply at all Miami-Dade County locations.</p>

                <img src="../images/counties/county-miami-dade-clerk-records-page-court-records.jpg" alt="Miami-Dade County court records search and registration page" loading="lazy">

                <p>You can also file documents and pay fees through the <a href="https://www.myflcourtaccess.com/">Florida Courts E-Filing Portal</a>. The portal runs around the clock for Miami-Dade County filings. Pursuant to Supreme Court Administrative Order AOSC 16-14, the clerk can now post many public court document images online for viewing. Cases and documents sealed by statute or court order stay out of public view in Miami-Dade County.</p>

                <p>Note: Record searches are limited to the first 200 results for online queries in Miami-Dade County.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- Criminal Court Section -->
            <section class="content-section">
                <h2>Miami-Dade Criminal Court Records</h2>
                <p>The Miami-Dade Criminal Court has two levels: Circuit Criminal and County Criminal. Circuit Criminal handles all felony cases, grand jury indictments, and arrest warrants. County Criminal deals with misdemeanor cases filed in Miami-Dade County. Misdemeanor jail arraignments are held Monday through Friday at 9 a.m. and 1:30 p.m. in Courtroom 5-3. On weekends and holidays, arraignments run once at 9 a.m.</p>

                <p>For felony case records in Miami-Dade County, contact the Criminal Division at the Richard E. Gerstein Justice Building, 1351 NW 12th St., Suite 9000, Miami, Florida 33125. Misdemeanor records are in Suite 8100 at the same building. Traffic court records are in Suite 124. You can search all of these through the COIN system on the <a href="https://www.miamidadeclerk.gov/clerk/criminal-court.page">clerk's criminal court page</a>. Bookmark cases to get alerts on updates to court records in Miami-Dade County.</p>

                <img src="../images/counties/county-miami-dade-clerk-criminal-court-page-court-records.jpg" alt="Miami-Dade County criminal court records and case information" loading="lazy">

                <p>Forms on the clerk's site are for reference only. The Miami-Dade Clerk cannot give legal advice or help fill out forms. Under Florida law, email addresses sent to the clerk are public records in Miami-Dade County. Be aware of jury duty scam emails that ask for payment or personal data.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- Additional Services Section -->
            <section class="content-section">
                <h2>Court Records Services in Miami-Dade County</h2>
                <p>The <a href="https://www.miamidadeclerk.gov/clerk/certified-copies.page">Miami-Dade Clerk</a> offers certified copies and e-certified documents. You can order them online or in person. The <a href="https://www2.miamidadeclerk.gov/trafficapp/AllSearches.aspx">traffic citations search</a> lets you look up and pay tickets from home in Miami-Dade County. A <a href="https://www2.miamidadeclerk.gov/mlsweb/LicenseSearch">marriage license search</a> is also available for court records in the county.</p>

                <p>The <a href="https://www.flclerks.com/">Florida Court Clerks & Comptrollers</a> association connects all 67 county clerks in the state and offers public services. You can pay traffic fines, search official records, or check jury duty info through their site. The <a href="https://www.myfloridacounty.com/">MyFloridaCounty.com</a> portal lets you make payments for court fees in Miami-Dade County. The <a href="https://help.flcourts.gov/">Florida Courts Help</a> center has free forms and self-help guides for people who need to file on their own. All court forms are free from the Florida Supreme Court.</p>

                <p>Under Florida Rule of Judicial Administration 2.420, the public has access to all judicial branch records except those shielded by specific rules. Court records in Miami-Dade County include the case file, the progress docket, and all documents that track activity in a case. Trial court memoranda and draft opinions are exempt from public view. Registered user agreements must be notarized before enhanced access to court records is granted in Miami-Dade County.</p>
            </section>

